ocations), and `total` (integer). The algorithm: allocate exact budget across segments, each segment has a name, importance, minimum, capacity. Start with each at its minimum. Then while remaining budget > 0, allocate one token to the segment with highest importance/(next allocation position+1?) Actually importance divided by its next allocated position. The description says \"importance divided by its next allocated position\". That is, for a segment currently allocated x tokens, the next token would be at position x+1 (since positions 1-indexed?). The ratio is importance / (x+1). Give to max ratio, break ties by original order. Continue until budget exhausted or capacities reached (i.e., no segment can take more). If budget is insufficient to cover minima (sum minima > budget) or budget exceeds sum of capacities, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso note: \"conserved total\" should equal the budget? Or the sum of allocations? The schema says `total` is integer. Likely the total allocated tokens (should equal budget if feasible). So return total = sum allocations. Also `by_name` mapping from segment name to its allocation. `allocations` list in order of segments? The schema says `allocations` is array of integers. It doesn't specify order but likely corresponds to the order of `segments` array in the input. The dynamicKeysFrom indicates `by_name` keys are from `case.segments[*].name`, so we need to extract names from input.\n\nEdge cases: tie breaking by original segment order (the order they appear in the `segments` array). Also note: the budget is exact total. The algorithm: start with min for each. Determine remaining budget = budget - sum minima. If remaining < 0 -> infeasible. Then while remaining > 0, for each segment, compute if it can take more (allocation < capacity). Compute ratio = importance / (current_allocation+1). Find segment with max ratio, tie break by index order. Allocate 1 token to that segment, increase its allocation, decrease remaining budget. Continue